If \eqref{eq:10} has a local Liouvillian first integral, then there
is a Liouvillian first integral of the form 
\begin{equation} \omega(x_1,x_2) = \int_{(x_1^0,x_2^0)}^{(x_1,x_2)}
R\,X_2\,d x_1 - R\,X_1\,d x_2 
\end{equation}
 where
\begin{equation} R = \exp\left[\int_{(x_1^0,x_2^0)}^{(x_1,x_2)} U \mathrm{d}
x_1 + V \mathrm{d} x_2\right], 
\end{equation} 
with $U$ and $V$ are
rational functions in $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that
$$\partial
U/\partial x_2 =
\partial V/\partial x_1.$$

